2. \( a - c \cdot b = 0 \)

Explain how CORDIC multiplication for the multiplication \( z = x \cdot y \) works:

The unknown value for \( z \) is found by driving \( x \) to zero, bit by bit. If the \( i^{\text{th}} \) bit of \( x \) is non-zero, \( y_i \) is right-shifted by \( i \) bits and added to the current \( z \) value. The \( i^{\text{th}} \) bit is then removed from \( x \) by subtracting \( 2^{-i} \). For negative \( x \), adding \( 2^{-i} \) removes the \( i^{\text{th}} \) bit in two's complement format. When \( x \) reaches zero, all bits have been examined and \( z \) contains the signed product of \( x \) and \( y \) correct to \( B \) bits.

This algorithm differs from the standard shift-and-add multiplication in two ways:

1. Arithmetic right shifts are used instead of left shifts, accommodating signed numbers.
2. Computing the product to \( B \) bits using CORDIC is equivalent to rounding the result of the standard algorithm to the most significant \( B \) bits.

